= Knowledge lifecycle =

BITwiki is the durable end of a recursive knowledge-production loop, not the place where every unreviewed thought is dumped.

== Core loop ==
<pre>
question / source / task
        ↓
BIThub live work
        ↓
research / discussion / testing
        ↓
BITCORE process
Constructs · agents · COREs · Nodes · Workspaces · tools · humans
        ↓
reusable output / artifact
        ↓
review · evidence check · correction · structuring
        ↓
stable knowledge
        ↓
BITwiki
        ↓
future humans and agents reuse it
        ↺
new questions return to BIThub
</pre>

== Raw signal ==
Live work can begin as a question, source, observation, hypothesis, experiment, extended note, dispute, workflow output, or conceptual chaos. The first obligation is to preserve what was actually supplied before transforming it.

== Structured work ==
BIThub is where knowledge can be explored, debated, tested, corrected, and shaped. Complex tasks may be divided among scoped agents or workflows. Division of work is useful only when provenance and responsibility remain legible.

== Artifact ==
An artifact is a reusable output useful beyond the original interaction: research notes, reports, datasets, diagrams, workflows, templates, prompts, guides, tested procedures, reviewed summaries. An artifact is not automatically canon.

== Crystallization ==
Moving material into BITwiki means crystallizing discussion, research, or artifacts into structured encyclopedic/reference knowledge.

A good candidate is structured, modular, succinct without losing necessary depth, source-backed, useful beyond one thread, reference-oriented, linkable/queryable/reusable, and stable enough to represent the current accepted version.

A discussion should move into BITwiki only after the research is conducted properly, reviewed, and accepted as worth preserving.

== Recursion ==
A BITwiki page may reveal missing evidence, contradictions, implementation failures, stale facts, or new questions. Those can return to BIThub and the research layer. Recursive intelligence here means improvement through structured feedback loops, not infinite elaboration.

== Fidelity ==
The system should '''maintain fidelity in the knowledge continuum'''. Transformation can improve organization and legibility, but should not silently change the source claim, erase disagreement, hide uncertainty, or replace authored thought with generic synthesis.

